      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm looking into some premature longitudinal cracking on a new asphalt section we laid in Phoenix recently. Can you give me a summary of the common causes for this kind of pavement failure, specifically looking at thermal expansion and bitumen issues?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly. Premature longitudinal cracking in asphalt often occurs due to inadequate aggregate interlock, resulting in weakened pavement structure. Thermal expansion can exacerbate this issue as asphalt expands and contracts, leading to stress and eventual cracking. Bitumen quality, including its viscosity and adhesive properties, affects proper bonding with aggregates. Both factors can lead to a compromised pavement that fails sooner than expected. Under Phoenix's hot conditions, these issues are especially pronounced. Regular maintenance and ensuring proper mixture specifications can mitigate these effects.",
