    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. I'm also curious about the subgrade. We're worried the compaction wasn't sufficient before the pour. How much does subgrade instability contribute to these longitudinal cracks compared to the actual mix properties?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Subgrade instability is a significant factor that can lead to premature longitudinal cracking. If proper compaction isn't achieved during the laying process, the subgrade will not provide a stable foundation, resulting in uneven stress distribution and instability over time. This instability can result in subsidence, leading to deformation and cracking of the asphalt layers. While it's true that mix properties play a crucial role, an unstable subgrade is often the primary culprit. Adequate density and strength of the compaction must be ensured, and regular testing of the subgrade before pour can help predict and improve long-term pavement performance and integrity.",
