      "content": "Absolutely, external environmental factors and localized soil movement, such as frost action, root growth, or earth movements, can influence the formation of longitudinal cracks, leading to patterns that may not align with initial concerns. In colder climates, frost heave can cause significant upheaval affecting the pavement structure, while in hot climates like Phoenix, thermal stresses often localize the crack patterns. Moreover, areas with dense vegetation may experience root heave, which can lead to significant vertical displacement. It's critical to conduct a thorough investigation, including soil studies and vegetation assessments, to determine if any localized influences are contributing to the cracking pattern observed. Whether these issues occurred prior to construction or due to later environmental events will require a comprehensive half-life analysis of the subgrade and soil as well as historical environmental data of the area.",
